G2-009: explicit dependency contract / no implicit std.core prelude
- Remove automatic `std.core` resolution from the user-source resolver.
- Keep standard C++ dependencies owned by explicit source modules and quoted `use` directives.
- Add native integration tests for header-free programs, explicit `std.core`, and `use "cstdint"`.
- Verify the no-import path still builds by mapping i64 to the GNU-compatible compiler's exact 64-bit type without injecting `<cstdint>`.
- Keep `std.core` minimal: it imports only `<string>` and `<vector>`; users opt into `<cstdint>` or other native headers separately when needed.
- Preserve the frozen Gen0/Gen1 compiler chain; Gen2 remains the actively developed compiler.

Next:
- Add semantic diagnostics when code uses string/List representations without importing a library that provides their C++ dependencies.
- Decide whether `std.core` should remain a small target-representation module or be split into more focused `std.text` / `std.containers` dependencies.
- Continue build-time definitions and macro design only after the dependency contract is covered.
